

From: Kyrylo Tkachov <ktkachov@nvidia.com>

The copies of a tablejump can share one jump table only if every copy
reaches the table and computes the same destinations from it.  Most ports
address the table through its label, with absolute entries or entries
relative to that label, and need nothing more.  Some dispatch sequences
depend on the table following the jump instead.

Add TARGET_CAN_SHARE_JUMP_TABLE_P so that a port can refuse sharing for a
particular dispatch under the current function's target options.  It is
queried before duplication only.  It defaults to true.  A port that cannot
share tables at all defines it as the new hook_bool_rtx_insn_false.  Output
and late verification do not query it.

AArch64 addr_diff_vec entries are relative to a synthetic text label.  The
casesi output sequence currently defines that label at each dispatch.  If
several dispatches share one table, they define the same assembler symbol more
than once.

The base label belongs to the table:

  dispatch A --\
  dispatch B ----> .LrtxN: -> table N
  dispatch C --/

Add TARGET_ASM_JUMP_TABLE_BASE and call it once per shared table while final
still has the function section selected.  Only a port whose dispatch output
would otherwise define the base of the entries needs it.

Preserve the existing AArch64 path for an unshared table.  Its casesi output
still defines the base label and uses ADR.  Once a table is shared, the hook
emits the base label and each dispatch refers to it.  Shared copies can be more
than ADR's range from the base label, so use ADRP and ADD outside the tiny code
model and account for the extra instruction in the length attribute.

No pass creates shared tables in this patch.
Ok for trunk?
Thanks,
Kyrill

